{"title":"American Invitational Mathematics Examination","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>The <b>American Invitational Mathematics Examination</b> (<b>AIME</b>) is the second exam in the series of exams used to challenge bright students on the path toward choosing the team that represents the United States at the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=International_Mathematics_Olympiad\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"International Mathematics Olympiad\">International Mathematics Olympiad</a> (IMO).  While most AIME participants are high school students, some bright middle school students also qualify each year.\n</p><p>High scoring AIME students are invited to take the prestigious <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=United_States_of_America_Mathematics_Olympiad\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"United States of America Mathematics Olympiad\">United States of America Mathematics Olympiad</a> (USAMO) for qualification from taking the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_12\" title=\"AMC 12\">AMC 12</a> or <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=United_States_of_America_Junior_Mathematical_Olympiad\" title=\"United States of America Junior Mathematical Olympiad\">United States of America Junior Mathematical Olympiad</a> (USAJMO) for qualification from taking the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_10\" title=\"AMC 10\">AMC 10</a>. <b>Note that although one may qualify for the AIME using the AMC 12 and AMC 10, as of the current school year, the AMC scores will not affect qualification for USAMO nor USAJMO.</b>\n</p><p>The AIME is administered by the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematical_Association_of_America\" title=\"Mathematical Association of America\">Mathematical Association of America</a> (MAA).  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Art_of_Problem_Solving\" title=\"Art of Problem Solving\">Art of Problem Solving</a> (AoPS) is a proud sponsor of the AMC.\n</p>\n<table style=\"border:thin solid black;padding:10px;float:right;margin:10px;background:#fff;\">\n\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"border:thin solid gray;text-align:center;background:#ccf;\"><b>AIME</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td><b>Region</b>: USA\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td><b>Type</b>: Free Response\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AoPS_Wiki:Competition_ratings\" title=\"AoPS Wiki:Competition ratings\">Difficulty</a></b>: 3-6\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td><b>Difficulty Breakdown:</b>\n<p><u>Problem 1-5</u>: 3<br /><u>Problem 6-9</u>: 4<br /><u>Problem 10-12</u>: 5<br /><u>Problem 13-15</u>: 6\n</p>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Format\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Format</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Qualification_Details\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Qualification Details</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#About_the_Exam\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">About the Exam</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Curriculum\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Curriculum</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Resources\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Resources</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Links\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Links</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Books_&amp;_Classes\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Books &amp; Classes</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Format\">Format</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=American_Invitational_Mathematics_Examination&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Format\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The AIME is a 15 question, 3 hour exam<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/d/9ed31dd48143a1ffe416cbccc2ab8972ecd9cc9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$^1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 7px\" width=\"5\" height=\"7\" /> taken by high scorers on the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_10\" title=\"AMC 10\">AMC 10</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_12\" title=\"AMC 12\">AMC 12</a>, and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMTS\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"USAMTS\">USAMTS</a> competitions, with qualification from the latter being rare. Before 2026-27, there were two dates for the AIME, and two AIME tests; the AIME 1 and the AIME 2. Now, however, there is only one testing date for the AIME.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Qualification_Details\">Qualification Details</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=American_Invitational_Mathematics_Examination&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Qualification Details\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Starting with the 2026-27 school season, cutoffs for qualifying for the AIME are now fixed at a specific number for the AMC 10 and AMC 12 competitions. A competitor must receive a score of 100 on the AMC 10, or a score of 85 on the AMC 12, to qualify for the AIME. Additionally, because AIMEs will be taken at Pearson testing centers, students under the age of 13 are not allowed to participate in the AIME. \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"About_the_Exam\">About the Exam</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=American_Invitational_Mathematics_Examination&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: About the Exam\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The exam is split into 2 parts; the first part consists of eight questions<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/0/2f095616666d4fcaba292be9fb5b5e79e96da54c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 7px\" width=\"6\" height=\"7\" />, to be taken in the time of 90 minutes, and the second part, which consists of the remaining seven questions<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/0/2f095616666d4fcaba292be9fb5b5e79e96da54c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 7px\" width=\"6\" height=\"7\" />, to be taken in the remaining 90 minutes. All of the AIME contests will be taken in approved Pearson testing centers, with all of the exams in a two-part, computerized format. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/0/2f095616666d4fcaba292be9fb5b5e79e96da54c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 7px\" width=\"6\" height=\"7\" /> \n</p><p>Each answer is an integer from 000 to 999, inclusive, making guessing almost futile.  Wrong answers receive no credit, while correct answers receive one point of credit, making the maximum score 15.  Problems generally increase in difficulty as the exam progresses - the first few questions are generally AMC 12 level, while the later questions become extremely difficult in comparison. Calculators are not permitted.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/d/9ed31dd48143a1ffe416cbccc2ab8972ecd9cc9a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$^1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 7px\" width=\"5\" height=\"7\" /> In the first two years (1983 and 1984) there was a 2.5 hour time limit instead of the current 3 hour limit.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/0/2f095616666d4fcaba292be9fb5b5e79e96da54c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 7px\" width=\"6\" height=\"7\" /> These changes are as of the 2026-27 school season.\n</p><p>High scores on the AIME lead to qualification to the prestigious USAMO and USAJMO olympiad proof competitions.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Curriculum\">Curriculum</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=American_Invitational_Mathematics_Examination&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Curriculum\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The AIME tests <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematical_problem_solving\" title=\"Mathematical problem solving\">mathematical problem solving</a> with <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Arithmetic\" title=\"Arithmetic\">arithmetic</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Algebra\" title=\"Algebra\">algebra</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Counting\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Counting\">counting</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Geometry\" title=\"Geometry\">geometry</a>, <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Number_theory\" title=\"Number theory\">number theory</a>, and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Probability\" title=\"Probability\">probability</a> and other secondary school math topics.  Problems usually require either very creative use of secondary school curriculum, or an understanding as to how different areas of math can be used together to investigate and solve a problem.  \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=American_Invitational_Mathematics_Examination&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Links\">Links</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=American_Invitational_Mathematics_Examination&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Links\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<ul><li>The MAA's <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://maa.org/student-programs/amc/\">official webpage</a> for the AMC and its <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://maa.org/maa-invitational-competitions/\">Invitational Competitions</a> page</li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//www.randommath.com/tournaments/aime\">Randommath.com</a>, a source for accessing AIME solutions immediately after the contest for people impatiently eager to know their score</li>\n<li>The AoPS Wiki's <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AIME_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AIME Problems and Solutions\">AIME Problems and Solutions</a> page</li>\n<li>The AoPS Contest Collection's <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3416_aime_problems\">AIME Problems</a> folder</li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/AMC_historical_results\">AoPS Wiki</a> and <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://maa.edvistas.com/eduview/report.aspx?mode=6&amp;view=1561\">MAA</a>'s statistics pages for past AIME exams</li>\n<li>AoPS <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mock_AIME\" title=\"Mock AIME\"> User created mock AIMEs</a>, an abundance of mocks designed to mimic real AIME tests, compiled <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c2439872_aime_mocks\">here</a> in AoPS Contests (some of the original mock pdf files are compiled <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c594864h3441744\">here</a>)</li>\n<li>Additional paid mock AIMEs by <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://ivyleaguecenter.org/mock-tests/\">Ivy League Education Center</a> (20 sets), <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/school/recommendations\">AoPS classes</a> (1-3 sets per class) <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/B08VYD6S42/artofproblems-20\">AlphaStar Academy</a> (4 sets), and <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/7ydJWdTV7UsxR2D3MNQS6A\">Macademy</a> (1 set)</li>\n<li>Past <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.stanfordmathtournament.com/past-tests/problems\">SMT</a>, <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.hmmt.org/www/archive/problems\">HMMT</a>, <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//pumac.princeton.edu/archives\">PUMaC</a>, <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//cmimc.com/archive\">CMIMC</a> and other <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3609896_usa__collegehosted_events\">college hosted contests</a></li>\n<li>Evan Chen's <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://blog.evanchen.cc/2019/01/31/math-contest-platitudes-v3/\">Math Contest Platitudes</a> blog and <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://web.evanchen.cc/faq-contest.html\">Math Contest FAQs</a></li>\n<li>BOGTRO's <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//www.dropbox.com/scl/fi/s0e887cmyh62fahkqwnqh/AIMElist.pdf?rlkey=csyo3vgdhd3b1mqkowl4gldy7&amp;e=1&amp;dl=0\">AIME Study guide</a></li>\n<li>Dylan Yu's <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//yu-dylan.github.io/files/handouts/M2-aime.pdf\">AIME Study guide</a>, accessed through his website <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://yu-dylan.github.io/handouts/\">here</a></li>\n<li>Collections of handouts by <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//artofproblemsolving.com/community/c5h1578191p15903900\">mathchampion1</a> (from 2018) and <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//artofproblemsolving.com/community/c1967976h2550333\">samrocksnature</a> (from 2021). Some of the links in these collections no longer work.</li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/user/902382\">zhaohm</a>'s <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c5\">folder</a> of past HMMT, PUMaC, AIME, IMO, etc. problems</li>\n<li>Omega Learn (Sohil Rathi)'s <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//www.youtube.com/@SohilRathi/featured\">youtube channel</a>, <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//www.omegalearn.org/aime\">AIME prep</a>, and <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//www.omegalearn.org/high-competition-math\">High School Competition Math</a> pages</li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//math.llmlab.io/\">Math.llmlab.io</a> and <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//ziml.areteem.org\">Zoom International Math League</a>, two online math training websites</li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//www.mathschool.com/blog/competitions/aime-problems-and-solutions\">RSM</a>'s compilation of official solution documents for past AIME problems</li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//drive.google.com/file/d/0B8JbOaFM5Xo_bnc2NUd0dDFLY1U/view?pref=2&amp;pli=AIME&amp;resourcekey=0-1tVMnKTLCkFlKuyljA_VyA\">This</a> handout of past AIME problems sorted by subject and difficulty</li></ul>\n<h3><span id=\"Books_.26_Classes\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Books_&amp;_Classes\">Books &amp; Classes</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=American_Invitational_Mathematics_Examination&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Books &amp; Classes\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<ul><li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//artofproblemsolving.com/store/book/aops-vol2\">The Art of Problem Solving Volume 2</a> by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Richard_Rusczyk\" title=\"Richard Rusczyk\">Richard Rusczyk</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Sandor_Lehoczky\" title=\"Sandor Lehoczky\">Sandor Lehoczky</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//artofproblemsolving.com/store/book/intermediate-algebra\">Intermediate Algebra</a> by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Richard_Rusczyk\" title=\"Richard Rusczyk\">Richard Rusczyk</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathew_Crawford\" title=\"Mathew Crawford\">Mathew Crawford</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//artofproblemsolving.com/store/book/intermediate-counting\">Intermediate Counting &amp; Probability</a> by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=David_Patrick\" title=\"David Patrick\">David Patrick</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//artofproblemsolving.com/store/book/precalculus\">Precalculus</a> by <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Richard_Rusczyk\" title=\"Richard Rusczyk\">Richard Rusczyk</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//bookstore.ams.org/view?ProductCode=PRB/26\">A Gentle Introduction to the American Invitational Mathematics Exam</a> by Scott Annin</li>\n<li>The AoPS <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//artofproblemsolving.com/school/schedule\">Online School</a>, with AIME prep classes and other classes on AIME topics</li>\n<li>The AoPS <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"//artofproblemsolving.com/school/mathjams\">Math Jams</a>, some of which are devoted to discussing AIME problems</li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=American_Invitational_Mathematics_Examination&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=American_Mathematics_Competitions\" title=\"American Mathematics Competitions\">American Mathematics Competitions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AIME_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AIME Problems and Solutions\">AIME Problems and Solutions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_historical_results\" title=\"AMC historical results\">AMC historical results</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Resources_for_mathematics_competitions\" title=\"Resources for mathematics competitions\">Resources for mathematics competitions</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=How_should_I_prepare%3F\" title=\"How should I prepare?\">How should I prepare?</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematics_Olympiads\" title=\"Mathematics Olympiads\">Mathematics Olympiads</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017075439\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.031 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.051 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 98/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 421/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 123/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 1014/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.459      1 Template:Contest_Info\n100.00%    6.459      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:1492:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017075439 and revision id 291232. Rendering was triggered be